<p>
    One year ago, in September 2025, a sample of experts was asked about the probability that AI would
    "solve or substantially assist in solving" a Millenium Prize Problem by 2027.
    The median answer was {{ externalLink("https://leap.forecastingresearch.org/reports/wave2#millennium-prize", "10%") -}}.
    One month ago, in September 2026, 
    {{ externalLink("https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/", "AI solved a Millenium Prize Problem") -}}.
</p>
<p>
    The {{ externalLink("https://www.claymath.org/millennium-problems/", "Millennium Prize Problems") }}
    are a set of seven well-known but difficult problems in mathematics. In the year 2000, each was given a $1 million bounty.
    These problems are the holy grails of mathematics research; the prestige of solving one is worth more than the $1 million prize.
    As of 2026, {{ externalLink("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poincar%C3%A9_conjecture", "one") }} has been solved by humans.
    And now, one has been solved by AI.
    {%- call sidenote("sn-solvedispute") %}
    There is {{ externalLink("https://www.science.org/content/article/how-ai-math-breakthrough-ignited-controversy", "dispute") }}
    over how much the AI solved on its own. 
    OpenAI heard rumors that someone associated with their chief rival Anthropic was about to solve the problem,
    so they sent an army of 10,000 AI agents at it, and successfully sniped it.
    The rival team was using OpenAI's Codex product, so OpenAI could have had access to their prompts.
    Regardless, without AI, the problem would remain unsolved today.
    {% endcall %}
</p>
<p>
    This shockingly early advancement has created a sense of existential crisis among mathematicians.
    What if ChatGPT is now the best mathematician in the world?
    In response, more than half of the living Fields medalists
    {%- call sidenote("sn-fieldsmedal") %}
    The Fields Medal is analogous to the Nobel Prize, but for math.
    {% endcall %}
    have now signed a {{ externalLink("https://mathandai.org/", "declaration") }}
    stating that using AI to autonomously extract answers for unsolved math problems is bad and should be stopped.
    They argue that the goal of mathematics is human understanding,
    and that the journey of solving a problem is more important than the destination of having an answer.
</p>
<p>
    I like to learn. I am completely satisfied with knowledge as an ultimate goal.
    So, I understand and agree with the mathematicians' perspective.
    But not everyone wants to be a lifetime student.
    Many kids wonder in math class, "Why do I need to know this?" "How will I use this in the real world?"
    To that sort of person, it doesn't matter how the solution was produced, only that it can be used.
    Who cares whether mathematicians found the solution intellectually satisfying?
</p>
<p>
    If AI can do math better than mathematicians, do we even need mathematicians anymore?
</p>

<h2>Do we even need smart people anymore?</h2>
<p>
    A little over a year ago, I created some simple problems for ARC-AGI-1, and tested them on the LLMs of the time.
    They were astonishingly bad at them. This gave me hope for the value of human intelligence.
    But Astra just nailed the ARC-AGI-3 benchmark.
</p>
<p>
    The general public is mostly unaware of how good AI has gotten,
    it is best at the things I am doing, and it is best at things that are verifiable.
    It remains unimpressive at tasks with no clear success state.
    The pelican benchmark is impressive if you consider that it was made by machine, but it is unimpressive as creative output.

    AI has also exceeded the ARC-AGI-3 benchmark.
    A year and a half ago, I made some problems for ARC-AGI-2, and the AI was absolutely terrible at it.
    This, I thought, is evidence that the LLM architecture is not enough to lead to AGI.
    But layers on top of the basic architecture, including chain of thought, better integration with tools, and coordination between agents,
    has made it so that AI is better than the average human on the abstract puzzle games of ARC-AGI-3.
    AI is better than the best human forecasters.
    AI is good at anything verifiable. Anything not verifiable depends on charisma

    Would AI make piss christ?

    An argument that is commonly made by the sorts of people who like to reduce every problem to capitalism
    is that AI isn't actually that good and it's only as widespread as it is because it's being shoved down our throats because profit.
    This argument frustrates me because it's the exact opposite of the problem. AI is <em>too</em> good!
</p>
<p>
    Intelligence itself is being automated.
    All tasks that require knowledge, problem-solving, and creativity will soon be done better and more efficiently with AI.
    People disagree on whether this will happen within one year or twenty years, but eventually, it will happen.
</p>
<p>
    It is not unusual for jobs to become obsolete.
    Many types of jobs have been automated since the start of the Industrial Revolution.
    What is unprecedented is the direction of the replacement, and the scope.
    Historically, it's been low-level, dumb jobs that get replaced.
    There are not many jobs anymore that involve doing repetitive manual labor.
    But AI threatens to automate away the smart jobs, and send us back to doing the manual labor.
    And since intelligence is so generally useful, it will automate away every intellectual pursuit at once.
</p>
<p>
    I'm a smart person. It is easiest for me to make myself valuable as an employee through my problem-solving ability.
    So what should I do? I have no idea.
    My primary value comes from my intelligence.
    I'm terrible with people. I have no charisma. Nobody wants to do my ideas.
    I'm in decent physical shape, but going to something I'm top 50% in from something I'm top 1% in feels terrible.
    
</p>

<h3>What's the point of learning marketable skills?</h3>
<p>
    Earlier this year, I was having fun learning about low-level programming.
    As a former front-end developer, learning C gave me an understanding of comptuers deeper than what I had before.
    I was reading The Linux Programming Interface straight through.
    I was learning Odin and had decided that it was the ultimate language that I wanted to make everything in forever.
    I found the handmade network and was inspired by their philosophy,
    even though intellectually I have always believed that getting things done was the most important aprt of programming.
    Making things yourself and learning is more fun.
    I was thinking about doing the recurse center.
    But now, I wonder, what's the point?
    AI is going to be better than me at anything I could learn.
    Programming knowledge is not like physics. It is not fundamental understanding of the universe.
    It is just understanding the constructs other humans have come up with to more easily represent the complexity of computers.
    I was going to include a dev manifesto on this site, but it feels pointless now.
</p>

<h2>Can we still play games?</h2>
<p>
    Perhaps games are the future. AI has been better than humans at chess for a long time,
    but chess reached a peak popularity in Covid higher than it ever was.
    The things I've done this year that have value to someone (even if they ahve no monetary value)
    are this website, and my GTNH videos.
</p>
